id: pm-web-pi1i
title: Prove that a write arriving from another surface reaches a connected subscriber
description: "pm-web, pm-gpt and the pm-mcp endpoint mount the same host directory, so an item edited through one surface is a plain filesystem write as far as the others are concerned. That shared mount establishes storage topology. It does not establish delivery, and nothing asserted delivery.\n\nThe pieces were each well covered and the SEAM BETWEEN THEM was not. createProjectWatchCycle resolves its sink as 'deps.emit ?? deliverProjectEvent', and all twelve existing watcher tests inject deps.emit - so the default binding, the only one production uses, was never executed by any test. Renaming or rebinding it would have left the entire suite green while the hosted service silently stopped delivering.\n\nFour cases now drive the real cycle against a real directory, without injecting emit, and read what a real subscriber's response object receives: an out-of-band write is delivered with source filesystem; a first observation baselines rather than announcing a change that did not happen; a subscriber on a different project is NOT told, because a hosted service that broadcast every change to every subscriber would leak the existence and timing of one tenant's edits to another; and delivery through the default sink also records the mutation signal, which is what stops the next tick re-announcing a change this process already made.\n\nProven not vacuous against two defects. Replacing the default sink with a no-op fails 2 of 4. Making delivery unscoped, so every subscriber receives every project's events, fails the isolation case."
type: Feature
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: hosted,realtime,sse
created_at: "2026-09-07T14:46:12.537Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-07T14:46:13.487Z"
